<h3 class="tex-subsection" id="subsection:fully-faithful"><span data-tag="01JG">4.8.1</span> Essentially Surjective Functors</h3>
<p>
The notion of essentially surjective functor has an obvious $\infty $-categorical counterpart: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="01JQ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01JQ">Definition <span data-tag="01JQ">4.8.1.1</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories. We say that $F$ is <em>essentially surjective</em> if, for every object $D \in \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, there exists an object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ such that $F(C)$ is isomorphic to $D$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="01JU">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01JU">Example <span data-tag="01JU">4.8.1.2</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor between ordinary categories. Then $F$ is essentially surjective (in the sense of classical category theory) if and only if the induced map $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(F): \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$ is an essentially surjective functor of $\infty $-categories (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="01JQ" href="/tag/01JQ">4.8.1.1</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="01JS">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01JS">Remark <span data-tag="01JS">4.8.1.3</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor between $\infty $-categories. Then $F$ is essentially surjective if and only if the induced functor of homotopy categories $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{F}: \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \rightarrow \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}$ is essentially surjective (in the sense of classical category theory). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="01JV">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01JV">Example <span data-tag="01JV">4.8.1.4</span>.</a> Let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism of Kan complexes. Then $f$ is essentially surjective (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="01JQ" href="/tag/01JQ">4.8.1.1</a>) if and only if the induced map $\pi _0(f): \pi _0(X) \rightarrow \pi _0(Y)$ is a surjection. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="01JT">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01JT">Remark <span data-tag="01JT">4.8.1.5</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor between $\infty $-categories. Then $F$ is essentially surjective if and only if the induced map of Kan complexes $F^{\simeq }: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}^{\simeq }$ is essentially surjective. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06S0">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06S0">Remark <span data-tag="06S0">4.8.1.6</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories. If $F$ is surjective on objects, then it is essentially surjective. The converse holds if $F$ is an isofibration. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="01JW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01JW">Remark <span data-tag="01JW">4.8.1.7</span> <span class="named">(Transitivity)</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ and $G: \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ be functors of $\infty $-categories. Then: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
If $F$ and $G$ are essentially surjective, then the composite functor $G \circ F$ is essentially surjective. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $G \circ F$ is essentially surjective, then $G$ is essentially surjective. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $G \circ F$ is essentially surjective and $G$ is an equivalence of $\infty $-categories, then $F$ is essentially surjective. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06S1">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06S1">Remark <span data-tag="06S1">4.8.1.8</span> <span class="named">(Isomorphism Invariance)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be $\infty $-categories and let $F,G: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be functors which are isomorphic (as objects of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$). Then $F$ is essentially surjective if and only if $G$ is essentially surjective. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="0573">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0573">Remark <span data-tag="0573">4.8.1.9</span>.</a> Suppose we are given a categorical pullback diagram of $\infty $-categories </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\ar [r]^-{F} \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\ar [d] \\ \overline{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \ar [r]^-{\overline{F}} &amp;  \overline{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}. } \]
</div>
<p> If $\overline{F}$ is essentially surjective, then $F$ is essentially surjective. This follows from Proposition <a data-tag="033C" href="/tag/033C">4.5.3.15</a> and Corollary <a data-tag="051E" href="/tag/051E">3.5.1.24</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="01PE">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01PE">Remark <span data-tag="01PE">4.8.1.10</span>.</a> Suppose we are given a commutative diagram of $\infty $-categories </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\ar [r]^-{F} \ar [d]^{U} &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\ar [d]^{V} \\ \overline{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \ar [r]^-{\overline{F}} &amp;  \overline{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}. } \]
</div>
<p> satisfying the following conditions: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $U$ is an inner fibration and $V$ is an isofibration. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $\overline{F}$ is essentially surjective. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(c)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For each object $\overline{C} \in \overline{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$, the induced functor $F_{X}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{C} } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_{ \overline{F}( \overline{C} ) }$ is essentially surjective. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> Then the functor $F$ is essentially surjective. To prove this, consider an arbitrary object $D \in \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. Assumption $(b)$ guarantees that there exists an object $\overline{C} \in \overline{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ and an isomorphism $\overline{u}: \overline{F}(\overline{C}) \rightarrow V(D)$ in the $\infty $-category $\overline{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}$. Assumption $(a)$ guarantees that we can lift $\overline{u}$ to an isomorphism $u: D' \rightarrow D$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, where $D'$ belongs to the fiber $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_{ \overline{F}( \overline{C} )}$. Applying $(c)$, we can choose an object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{C} }$ and an isomorphism $v: F(C) \rightarrow D'$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_{ \overline{F}( \overline{C} ) }$. It follows that $D$ is isomorphic to $F(C)$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="06S2">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06S2">Definition <span data-tag="06S2">4.8.1.11</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories. The <em>essential image</em> of $F$ is the full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}' \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ spanned by those objects $D \in \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ which are isomorphic to $F(C)$, for some object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="01JR">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01JR">Remark <span data-tag="01JR">4.8.1.12</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories. The essential image of $F$ is a full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}' \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ which is uniquely determined by the following properties: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}' \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is replete: that is, it is closed under isomorphism (see Example <a data-tag="01EX" href="/tag/01EX">4.4.1.12</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The functor $F$ factors through $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}'$, and can therefore be identified with a functor $F': \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}'$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The functor $F'$ is essentially surjective. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06S3">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06S3">Remark <span data-tag="06S3">4.8.1.13</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories. Then $F$ is essentially surjective if and only if the essential image of $F$ coincides with $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
